NAME
    usb-install.sh - interactively flash and provision a complete m-boot disk

SYNOPSIS
    ./usb-install.sh

DESCRIPTION
    One command installs the complete BIOS x86_64, UEFI x86_64 and UEFI
    ARM64 m-boot image. It lists physical disks with numbers, prompts for
    a selection, displays the selected disk (including partition layout,
    filesystem and volume labels, model, serial and size when reported),
    and requires the operator to type ERASE followed by the exact selected
    device identifier. There are no device operands and no default target.

    AFTER CONFIRMATION it writes the verified image to the selected whole
    disk using dd, moves the backup GPT to the device end, keeps the BIOS
    and EFI partition geometry fixed, recreates partition 3 to occupy all
    remaining usable sectors, formats partition 3 as exFAT and restores
    the entire loader. All old data on the selected disk is destroyed.
    This is a one-time disk provisioning operation, NOT an update method
    for a data-bearing m-boot disk or an ISO installation command.

PUBLIC DISTRIBUTION
    GitHub Actions workflow .github/workflows/build-usb-image.yml runs
    the real build on Ubuntu 24.04 x86_64 and, after verification, commits
    the public, downloadable image to the m-boot repository:

        images/m-boot-combined.raw.gz
        images/m-boot-loader.zip
        images/SHA256SUMS
        images/RAW-SHA256SUMS
        images/BUILD-INFO.txt

    No GitHub Actions account or expiring artifact is needed for a normal
    public git clone or pull. The workflow also keeps its existing
    downloadable run artifact for troubleshooting. Image publication is a
    forward Git commit and can fail on a concurrent remote update rather
    than overwriting other commits. A failed build does not publish a new
    image. BUILD-INFO identifies the source commit, not the publication
    commit. Rebuilding may change binary provenance and Git blob IDs.

PLATFORMS AND REQUIREMENTS
    Install/build runtime: standalone. Linux and macOS supported by the
    user-facing script; GRUB is not built on the selected operator host.
    The logical sector size must be 512 bytes and disk capacity at least
    2 GiB. External physical devices are the initial eligible set; mounted
    Linux partitions and swaps are refused, and macOS unmounts the selected
    disk before writing. The entire selected device is destroyed, not
    merely its existing exFAT partition. Partitions cannot be silently
    identified by a guessed /dev/sdX or diskN.

    The script requires POSIX sh, gzip, unzip, checksumming tools and
    administrator authorization. Linux additionally needs lsblk,
    gdisk/sgdisk, partprobe, udevadm and exfatprogs. macOS needs diskutil,
    plutil, newfs_exfat, fsck_exfat and sgdisk from gdisk (typically
    available using Homebrew). All dependencies are checked BEFORE
    any physical disk write. A missing tool or stale kernel partition
    map stops safely with a diagnostic rather than guessing a device.

BOOT MEDIA GEOMETRY (512-BYTE LOGICAL SECTORS)
    Partition 1: EF02, LBAs 2048..22527 (10 MiB BIOS Boot, no filesystem)
    Partition 2: EF00, LBAs 22528..1071103 (512 MiB FAT32 ESP)
    Partition 3: 0700, starts at LBA 1071104 (exFAT common loader/data)

    The installed firmware executables are EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI and
    EFI/BOOT/BOOTAA64.EFI on the same ESP, with complete GRUB runtime
    modules for all three targets under /m/boot/loader on exFAT.
    The third partition's original exFAT content is discarded by design:
    images/m-boot-loader.zip is restored after reformatting. Fresh GPT
    disk and partition GUIDs are generated to avoid duplicate media IDs.

USE
    git clone https://github.com/massimilianonardi-ai/m-boot.git
    cd m-boot
    ./usb-install.sh

    The installer verifies both compressed-distribution and decompressed
    raw-disk SHA256, stages the image and loader outside the target disk,
    lists numbered disks, shows full selected-device details, and waits
    for typed authorization. No manual dd, GPT edit or filesystem
    expansion commands are necessary after selection.

VALIDATION LIMITS
    GitHub CI validates real GRUB image construction, GPT preparation,
    publication archive checks and a real loop-backed larger-disk exFAT
    restoration. A CI test image is not a physical USB/Thunderbolt disk.
    Booting actual devices with BIOS x86_64, UEFI x86_64 and UEFI ARM64,
    and running the interactive physical-target installer on both hosts,
    remain separate physical validation obligations. The workflow does
    not claim Secure Boot, Apple Silicon boot or arbitrary ISO support.

